<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yi I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a>–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Yulgok</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yi I, known as Yulgok, was one of the two greatest Neo-Confucian philosophers of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a>. The son of the artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shin-saimdang">Sin Saimdang</a>, he passed the civil service examinations at the top nine times and held high office. He stressed the unity of principle and material force and urged practical reforms of taxation and defence. He appears on the South Korean 5,000-won note.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yi I was born in Gangneung, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangwon-province">Gangwon Province</a>, in 1536 into the Deoksu Yi clan. His father, Yi Won-su, was a Left Second State Councillor, and his mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shin-saimdang">Shin Saimdang</a>, was an accomplished artist and calligrapher. He was the grandnephew of Yi Ki, Chief State Councilor from 1549 to 1551. He studied under Baek In-geol, successor of Cho Kwangjo, and is said to have finished his lessons in the Confucian classics by seven and passed the Civil Service literary examination at 13.</p><p>After his mother's death when he was 16 he secluded himself on Kumgang-san for three years, studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He left the mountains at 20 and devoted himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>. He married at 22 and the next year visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a> at Dosan. His father died when he was 26.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He passed special examinations with top honours with a thesis called Cheondochaek, widely regarded as a literary masterpiece that displayed his knowledge of history, Confucian political philosophy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>. He took top honours in civil examinations nine times in a row. He held government posts from the age of 29, went on a mission in 1568 as a document officer, took part in writing the Myeongjong Annals, and at 34 wrote Dongho Mundap, an eleven-article political memorial arguing that righteous government could be achieved.</p><p>By about 40 he had the deep trust of the king and was one of the central political figures. When political conflicts escalated in 1576 his efforts proved fruitless and he returned home to study and teach. He came back to office at 45 and held several ministerial posts, writing works that recorded crucial events and tried to ease the conflicts of the time. King Seonjo remained noncommittal, and Yi found it hard to stay neutral. He left office in 1583 and died the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Yi did not fully agree with the dualistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a>. His school stressed concrete, material elements and external experience and learning rather than inner spiritual perception. Unlike Yi Hwang, who disliked politics, he was an active official who wanted Confucian principles applied to administration, with sage learning and self-cultivation as its base. He proposed raising and strengthening an army against a possible Japanese attack, but the central government rejected it, and he died before the Imjin war began.</p><p>His works include Questions and Answers at East Lake, the Memorial in Ten Thousand Words, The Essentials of the Studies of the Sages, The Secret of Expelling Ignorance and the Daily Records of Lectures before the Throne. The Complete Works of Yulgok was compiled after his death. A street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, Yulgongno, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yi I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Neo-Confucian philosophy reached a high level in the debates of Yi Hwang (Toegye) and <b>Yi I</b> (Yulgok), whose ideas also influenced Japan, and later scholars of Practical Learning (silhak) such as Jeong Yak-yong turned to the reform of land, administration and technology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Korea, scholars such as Yi Hwang and <b>Yi I</b> debated Neo-Confucian metaphysics in the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Some won lasting fame: the painter Sin Saimdang, mother of the philosopher <b>Yi I</b>; the gisaeng poet Hwang Jini; and the merchant Kim Man-deok, who gave her rice to the starving.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gangneung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Hanseongbu, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.76,128.9&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Gangneung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7767 806 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yi I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8217 1076h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8217 1076h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.76,128.9&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
